WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.280
Containment is the work of limiting damage while preserving enough evidence and business

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.500
continuity to make good decisions.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.880
It is one of the hardest parts of incident response because the best action depends on

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:11.980
timing,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.840
confidence, impact, and risk.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.820
A playbook should give responders containment options and decision criteria,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.200
not just a single command to run every time.

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.420
Common containment options include isolating a host, disabling an account,

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:31.240
revoking tokens, blocking an indicator, quarantining email, restricting a network path,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:36.740
pausing an exposed service, removing public access, or temporarily limiting a role.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.080
Each action has tradeoffs. Isolating a host may stop activity but can disrupt business

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.940
work and may change volatile evidence.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.600
Disabling an account may prevent unauthorized access but can affect critical service

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.940
operations if the account is used by automation.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.240
Blocking an indicator may reduce noise but may not address the root cause.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.260
Pausing an exposed service may protect data but can affect customers. Premature

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:05.720
containment can destroy context.

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.420
If responders immediately power off a system, they may lose memory, network state,

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.080
active process information, or live session details.

00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:17.660
If they remove every suspicious email before measuring scope,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.200
they may make it harder to know who received it.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.620
If they revoke a cloud key without capturing audit evidence,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.320
they may lose a clean view of what it accessed.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.940
This does not mean containment should be delayed by default. It means the playbook should

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.840
help responders decide what evidence is needed before action,

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.700
and when urgent risk overrides further collection.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.880
Delayed containment can increase impact. If a privileged account is actively changing

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.140
policies,

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.680
waiting too long can expose more resources.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.580
If a device is still communicating with known malicious infrastructure,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.480
isolation may be urgent.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:57.660
If sensitive data is publicly reachable, reducing exposure may be the first priority.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:02.120
The playbook should identify conditions that justify fast containment and the approvals

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.580
required when business impact is high.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:08.960
Eradication comes after the team understands enough to remove the attacker's foothold,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:14.300
malicious artifacts, persistence, unauthorized access, and exploited weakness.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:19.640
In defensive terms, eradication may mean removing malware, resetting credentials,

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:24.580
rotating keys, deleting unauthorized accounts, closing an exposed service path,

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.560
fixing a vulnerable application, removing a malicious mailbox rule,

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.980
or rebuilding a compromised host from a trusted image.

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.000
The key is that eradication addresses more than the symptom. Recovery restores systems

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:38.100
safely.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.720
The playbook should require verification: access reviewed, persistence removed,

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:48.620
logs checked, systems patched or rebuilt, data restored if needed, monitoring in place,

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.320
and owners ready to resume normal operation.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.860
Containment, eradication,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.960
and recovery are connected decisions. A strong playbook helps responders act quickly

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.860
without acting blindly.
